{"name":"Subterranean River","hex":"#1f3b4d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"subterranean","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Below ground, under the earth, underground.","Secret, concealed."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Someone or something that is subterranean."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/subterranean"},{"term":"river","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large and often winding stream which drains a land mass, carrying water down from higher areas to a lower point, oftentimes ending in another body of water, such as an ocean or in an inland sea.","Any large flow of a liquid in a single body."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To improve one’s hand to beat another player on the final card in a poker game."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/river"}],"entries":[{"title":"Subterranean river","blocks":[{"text":"A subterranean river is a river or watercourse that runs wholly or partly beneath the ground, one where the riverbed does not represent the surface of the Earth. It is distinct from an aquifer, which may flow like a river but is contained within a permeable layer of rock or other unconsolidated materials. A river flowing below ground level in an open gorge is not classed as subterranean."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Burger_OtokKriznaJama.jpg/330px-Burger_OtokKriznaJama.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subterranean_river"},{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Buna_source.jpg/500px-Buna_source.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Buna_source.jpg/1280px-Buna_source.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Buna_source.jpg/500px-Buna_source.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The cave of source of the Buna can be entered by boat and dived through a cave system serving as an effluence of the Zalomka.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABuna_source.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Caves_entranceexit.jpg/500px-Caves_entranceexit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Caves_entranceexit.jpg/500px-Caves_entranceexit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Puerto Princesa cave can be entered by boat.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACaves_entranceexit.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/La_chute_d%27entr%C3%A9e_de_la_Gorge_du_Diable.jpg/500px-La_chute_d%27entr%C3%A9e_de_la_Gorge_du_Diable.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&ut